              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "One major cause is the shortage of affordable housing in city centres, where most jobs are located. As demand grows, prices rise faster than salaries. To tackle this, local governments could require developers to include a percentage of low-cost flats in every new building, as some European cities already do.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Body 2:",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A second factor is the growth of short-term holiday rentals, which reduce the number of homes available to residents. This issue could be addressed by limiting the number of days per year that a property can be rented to tourists. If such rules were enforced, more flats would return to the long-term market.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
